@startuml
' THE PERSISTENCE HALF OF THIS DIAGRAM WAS FICTION UNTIL 2026-08-31. It showed the gateway producing
' a Kafka record consumed by a "Chat History Service" that has never existed in this repository.
' There was no such service, no such topic, and the broker was removed once it was measured empty.
' What is drawn below is the flow the code actually takes, per docs/wiki/architecture.md.
actor "Alice (Expediteur)" as UserA
participant "Client A (MLS)" as ClientA
participant "chat-delivery-service (DS)" as Delivery
participant "Redis (Stream + Pub/Sub)" as Redis
participant "chat-gateway" as Gateway
participant "Client B (MLS)" as ClientB

UserA -> ClientA: Tape "Bonjour"

note right of ClientA
  MLS Application Message
  1. Recuperation Etat Groupe
  2. Protect(plain_text) -> MLS Ciphertext
end note

ClientA -> Delivery: POST /api/mls/send
{proto, groupId, recipientId, deviceId}

note right of Delivery: chat-delivery-service agit
comme Delivery Service (DS)

Delivery -> Delivery: Validate Payload Size/Type

group Persistance
    Delivery -> Redis: XADD history:{groupId} (Blob MLS opaque)
end

group Distribution Temps Reel (fan-out)
    Delivery -> Redis: PUBLISH "chat:messages"
(un message par device destinataire)
    Redis -> Gateway: OnMessage
    Gateway -> Gateway: Lookup connected_users["userId:deviceId"]
    alt device connecte
        Gateway -> ClientB: WebSocket {proto, senderId, senderDeviceId, groupId}
    else device hors ligne
        note right of Gateway
          Rien n'est envoye. Le message reste
          dans la file et est recupere au
          retour par GET /api/mls/messages/
        end note
    end
end

ClientB -> ClientB: MLS Process(Ciphertext)
note right of ClientB
  Dechiffrement &
  Mise a jour Etat Groupe (Ratchet)
end note

ClientB --> UserA: (Optionnel) Delivery Receipt
@enduml
